<<<
+ qualified adjectivally by the other element
===
+ expression consisting of a noun qualified adjectivally by the other element
>>>

<<<
in this function the.hyphen is a recognition
===
in this function the hyphen is a recognition
>>>

<<<
in print of what has already happened in speech, i.e. that the compound
===
in print of what has already happened in speech, i. e. that the compound
>>>

<<<
(compare the accentuation of Thrushes are not black birds with that of Thrushes are not blackbirds or blackbirds).
===
(compare the accentuation of *Thrushes are not black birds* with that of *Thrushes are not black-birds* or *blackbirds*).
>>>

<<<
as in black-bird, red-coat) or an attributive noun
===
as in *black-bird, red-coat*) or an attributive noun
>>>

<<<
(as in waterrat).
===
(as in *water-rat*).
>>>

<<<
Thus Court Martial, if it is to have a possessive ease (the Court-martial's decision was ...), must be one word;
===
Thus *Court Martial*, if it is to have a possessive case (*the Court-martial's decision was . . .*), must be one word;
>>>

<<<
long legs, storms beat, rend hearts, carry weight, come home, handle by means of men, proof against bombs, spits fire, go by, can be converted
===
*long legs, storms beat, rend hearts, carry weight, come home, handle by means of men, proof against bombs, spits fire, go by,* can be converted
>>>

<<<
so we get longlegged, stormbeaten, heartrending, weight-carrier, homecoming, man-handle, bomb-proof, spitfire, & (the) go-by.
===
so we get *longlegged, stormbeaten, heart-rending, weight-carrier, homecoming, man-handle, bomb-proof, spitfire, & (the) go-by.*
>>>

<<<
separately to a noun (I saw a red hot face, i.e. one both red & hot), are not to be so applied
===
separately to a noun (*I saw a red hot face,* i. e. one both red & hot), are not to be so applied
>>>

<<<
compound idea (holding a red-hot poker); such
===
compound idea (*holding a red-hot poker*); such
>>>

<<<
+ heroic, bitter-sweet, forcible-feeble.
===
+ *are dark-blue, worldly-wise, mock-heroic, bitter-sweet, forcible-feeble.*
>>>

<<<
(you put up, not put-up, a job, but the result is a put-up job).
===
(you *put up*, not *put-up*, a job, but the result is a *put-up* job).
>>>

<<<
thus oft-repeated will usually be hyphened, & ill served usually not.
===
thus *oft-repeated* will usually be hyphened, & *ill served* usually not.
>>>

<<<
*Ha-, ante-, pre-, anti-, post-, &e., are often wrongly prefixed to unhyphened double expressions.
===
∗*Ex-, ante-, pre-, anti-, post-, &c.,* are often wrongly prefixed to unhyphened double expressions.
>>>

<<<
be circumvented; e.g., the first quotation
===
be circumvented; e. g., the first quotation
>>>

<<<
the first quotation should be corrected to opened the campaign against Home Rule.
===
the first quotation should be corrected to *opened the campaign against* ***Home Rule.***
>>>

<<<
Quotations: Mr Austen Chamber-
===
Quotations: *Mr Austen Chamber-*
>>>
